   230	
   231	### 2.6 Comparison and preservation of tip kinematic information
   232	
   233	| Criterion | (a) global | (b) IPW global | (c) $T_\kappa$ + pooled IPW | **(d) recommended** |
   234	|---|---|---|---|---|
   235	| Fixes P0/P1 quota drift (Issue #7 literal) | no | **yes** | yes | **yes** |
   236	| Fixes lever-arm anisotropy (brief tension 1) | no | no (§1.4) | yes if $\kappa$ matches | **yes** |
   237	| Per-configuration erank guarantee | none ($\ge 16\pi_k$) | none | none | **direct**: $\widehat U_k$ ↔ $\mathrm{PR}_k$, hinge at 13 |
   238	| Escapes Corollary 1b floor (≥ 0.745) | no | no | no | **yes** (Theorem 2) |
   239	| Tip and lever-arm semantics | raw | raw | bijective + $\lambda$ | bijective + $\lambda$ + anchor |
   240	| Deployment purity and budget | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ (0 params) | ✓ (0 deployed params) |
   241	| Stroke and capacity residual dispersion | unhandled | unhandled | unhandled | stratified loss |
   242	
   243	**Tip semantics.**
   244	- **Information is preserved.** $T_\kappa$ is a bijection on the kinematic manifold, and F1–F2 show that raw tip slots contain exactly angular kinematics × the static lever vector. So no lever-arm, tip-velocity or tip-momentum information is discarded: $p_{\mathrm{lin}} = m_{\mathrm{eff}} v_{\mathrm{tip}}$, with $\rho$ recovered from $\lambda$.
   245	- **Goal mode.** The upper layer still writes $g^{\mathrm{des}}$ in physical SI units, including tip targets. $T_\kappa$ maps them, so a 1 m/s tip-speed target becomes different $\omega/\Omega$ on short and long links, as it physically should.
   246	- **Decoding mode.** Physical tip quantities are recovered as $T_\kappa^{-1}(\text{probe}(z))$. The $\lambda$-anchor makes $\rho$ decodable from $z$ without consulting the unit descriptor. Spike gate G4 tests this on **unseen** geometry seeds.
   247	
   248	---
   249	
   250	## 3. Serious Alternative Considered and Rejected
   251	
   252	**Alternative: per-configuration output whitening.** Set $B_k(g) = 4\cdot\mathrm{normalize}(\widehat\Sigma_k^{-1/2}B(g))$, either post hoc after training or as a learned adapter. Each deployed unit stores one symmetric 16×16 matrix.
   253	
   254	*Why it is serious.* It makes $\Sigma_B^{(k)} = I$, and so $\mathrm{PR}_k = 16$, **by construction** for every configuration. It is cheap and needs no change to the training loss. Each deployed unit is a single configuration, so a single matrix suffices. It would trivially pass any erank-only gate.
   255	
   256	*Why it is rejected.*
   257	1. **Budget.** It adds 136 deployed parameters per unit, which violates the fixed 281,617 total.
   258	2. **It cannot create information, and it games the gate.** Suppose $\Sigma_B^{(k)}$ has 4 directions with eigenvalue 0.01. Whitening scales them ×10. Those directions hold whatever small, possibly noise-dominated variation $B$ produced. By Proposition 4, the effective Lipschitz constant grows by $\lambda_{\min}^{-1/2}$, so outcome perturbations of encoder-noise size produce large angular changes of $z$. Effective rank measures spread, not information. **Lesson adopted:** every erank gate in §5 is paired with decoding-accuracy and Lipschitz anti-gaming gates (G4, G6).
   259	3. **It does not touch Corollary 1b.** If $F$ is trained against a pooled reference, the 0.745 floor is unchanged, because whitening is a per-configuration linear map inside $V$ and Theorem 1 already optimizes over those. If it is trained against a configuration-matched reference, it is redundant with the stratified loss.
   260	4. **It fragments $z$ semantics across units.** The same physical outcome $g$ maps to different $z$ in different units through unit-specific rotations. That works against the cross-unit composition targeted by B04/H4.

